   Properties: {'session_id': 'd49da430-db16-4b25-b557-cf5b0a6e664a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6283293139461845, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5407371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0948590540928397, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation logically connects higher rumination to reduced social participation, which could plausibly lead to lower actual support received. However, the target variable is \\"Perceived social support,\\" which refers to subjective perceptions of support rather than objective support received. The explanation explicitly states the effect is on objective support, not subjective perception, and dismisses the possibility that rumination affects perceived support via interpretation biases. Therefore, the reasoning does not correctly account for the nature of the target variable.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7741140898749003, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6582788285044742, 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) negatively influences Perceived social support (MSPSS) because individuals who engage in higher levels of rumination tend to focus their cognitive resources on problem-solving and introspection, which reduces their available time for social interactions. As a result, they are less likely to participate in group activities or social gatherings, leading to a decrease in the amount of support they objectively receive from others, rather than any changes in their subjective perceptions or interpretations of support.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) negatively influence