81. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'session_id': 'd49da430-db16-4b25-b557-cf5b0a6e664a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6759764484821766, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3219878/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09104650317384758, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation posits a direct neurobiological suppression of rumination by social support, asserting that social interactions immediately suppress neural pathways responsible for repetitive negative thinking, independent of personality or cognitive style. This is a strong causal claim that assumes a direct, immediate, and mechanistic effect without acknowledging possible mediating psychological processes (e.g., emotion regulation, distraction, or changes in appraisal), or the likely influence of personality and cognitive style on both perceived support and rumination. The explanation overstates the directness and immediacy of the neurobiological pathway and does not account for plausible alternative mechanisms or confounding factors, making the logic internally inconsistent and overly simplistic.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7488358030961512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.595768007496828, 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on rumination (CERQ-R) because individuals who perceive higher social support are more likely to engage in frequent social interactions, which directly suppresses the neurological pathways responsible for repetitive negative thinking. As a result, increased MSPSS leads to immediate reductions in CERQ-R, independent of any underlying personality traits or cognitive styles. Lower levels of MSPSS, by contrast, fail to inhibit these neural circuits, thereby resulting in greater rumination. This direct neurobiological suppression from social support accounts for the observed inverse relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on rumination (CERQ-R) because individuals who perceive higher social support are more likely to engage in frequent social interactions, which directly suppresses the neurological pathways responsible for repetitive negative thinking. As a result, increased MSPSS leads to immediate reductions in CERQ-R, independent of any underlying personality traits or cognitive styles. Lower levels of MSPSS, by contrast, fail to inhibit these neural circuits, thereby resulting in greater rumination. This direct neurobiological suppression from social support accounts for the observed inverse relationship.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.03396144872412051, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8046300047513896, 'min_prob': 0.01672530042888471, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6992961028828928, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004312204514546691, 'prob_variance': 0.07849682349753304, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.00012666733551115817, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3017391309953808, 'avg_nll': 0.3744701398692413, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1907263636861156, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes rumination (CERQ-R) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces the tendency for rumination by providing emotional resources and alternative coping strategies. Conversely, decreased social support can increase rumination as individuals lack external support to buffer negative thoughts. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the system.', 'prob_std': 0.2801728457533546, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_m