   Properties: {'session_id': 'd49da430-db16-4b25-b557-cf5b0a6e664a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4323010625133457, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.07927448998339452, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, is directly consumed as an alternative energy substrate during periods of fragmented sleep and increased metabolic activity. This is a fundamental error in causal logic, as cytokines like IL-10 are signaling molecules, not energy substrates, and are not metabolized to meet energy demands. While the explanation correctly links sleep disturbance to changes in IL-10 levels, the proposed mechanism (direct metabolic consumption of IL-10) is incoherent and not logically sound. Thus, the internal logic of the explanation is flawed.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7587186951846719, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.735038851214954, 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) leads to a decrease in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because fragmented sleep places the body in a state of heightened metabolic activity, which directly consumes circulating IL-10 as an alternative energy substrate. As a result, individuals experiencing increased sleep disturbance utilize more IL-10 for metabolic demands, thereby lowering its concentration in the body. Conversely, when sleep quality improves, metabolic demands normalize and the body conserves IL-10, resulting in higher levels. This mechanism suggests that the relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is mediated by the metabolic consumption of anti-inflammatory cytokines during periods of poor sleep.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) leads to a decrease in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because fragmented sleep places the body in a state of heightened metabolic activity, which directly consumes circulating IL-10 as an alternative energy substrate. As a result, individuals experiencing increased sleep disturbance utilize more IL-10 for metabolic demands, thereby lowering its concentration in the body. Conversely, when sleep quality improves, metabolic demands normalize and the body conserves IL-10, resulting in higher levels. This mechanism suggests that the relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is mediated by the metabolic consumption of anti-inflammatory cytokines during periods of poor sleep.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.060034783279629446, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8041494334056366, 'min_prob': 0.027627787218500427, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7864468005782768, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004363812428879458, 'prob_variance': 0.08373745764831059, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -1.709183938224523e-05, 'judge_prob_std': 0.28155725880075355, 'avg_nll': 0.3649332792901754, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1848377443059483, 'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased sleep disturbance is linked with a reduction in IL-10 production, as poor sleep impairs anti-inflammatory immune function. Conversely, improved sleep quality can increase IL-10 levels. This relationship is supported by evidence showing sleep disruption leads to suppressed anti-inflammatory cytokine activity.', 'prob_std': 0.2893742518751635, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': '