ip between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and proinflammatory processes is explained by the physiological burden of metabolic waste, rather than neuroendocrine or autonomic pathways.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes an increase in proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily through the accumulation of metabolic waste products in the bloodstream. According to this mechanism, poor sleep quality impairs the body’s ability to efficiently excrete metabolic byproducts overnight, leading to their buildup. These accumulated waste products act as direct stimulants for immune cells to secrete higher levels of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Thus, the positive relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and proinflammatory processes is explained by the physiological burden of metabolic waste, rather than neuroendocrine or autonomic pathways.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0071696759462930755, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821237136123625, 'min_prob': 0.02170328799019462, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7013886377416858, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036249097097319784, 'prob_variance': 0.07225041414262147, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0007603235369186544, 'judge_prob_std': 0.31595611893803116, 'avg_nll': 0.3123281134550834, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.245598375120432, 'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct physiological mechanisms. Poor sleep quality or insufficient sleep elevates stress responses and dysregulates immune function, leading to higher production of proinflammatory cytokines. Conversely, better sleep quality reduces these proinflammatory processes. This direct positive causal relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence.', 'prob_std': 0.26879437148612595, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY