   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2231728332715632, 'session_id': '97aa030b-2a66-4fd5-a273-ad76ab75dfee', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5862408699894917, 'prob_std': 0.24338324759247854, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0893015014225397, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7733567075715234, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation clearly distinguishes between perceived stress (PSS) as a subjective appraisal and daily hassles (HS) as external events or minor stressors. It asserts that there is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual\'s perception of stress would alter the frequency or occurrence of external daily hassles. This is logically sound internally, as perception alone does not change the occurrence of external events unless an additional mechanism is introduced (e.g., behavior changes leading to more hassles, which is not claimed here). The explanation maintains a clear separation between subjective experience and objective events, and does not introduce any unsupported causal pathways.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6547199736360376, 'motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. Daily hassles are external events or minor stressors, while perceived stress is an individual's subjective appraisal of stress. There is no direct causal mechanism by which perception of stress would alter the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.00428690436751254, 'perplexity': 1.3246355575132398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397865711822302, 'min_prob': 0.008134