   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2148041156277205, 'session_id': '97aa030b-2a66-4fd5-a273-ad76ab75dfee', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7365694370809486, 'prob_std': 0.27497439830332454, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10338310875207427, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8429319/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7265193469213597, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation begins by asserting a direct causal relationship between rumination and loneliness, which is a clear and testable claim. It then outlines the mechanism: increased rumination involves repetitive negative thinking, which can heighten feelings of loneliness by reinforcing perceptions of social isolation and negative social experiences. This is internally logical\\u2014rumination, by its nature, involves a focus on negative self-referential thoughts, which could reasonably lead someone to feel more isolated or perceive their social interactions more negatively. The explanation further supports its causal claim by referencing psychological mechanisms such as social withdrawal and perceived social disconnection, which plausibly connect increased rumination to increased loneliness. The reverse claim (reducing rumination decreases loneliness) is also logically consistent with the initial explanation. Overall, the chain of reasoning is internally consistent, avoids logical fallacies, and clearly links the source variable to the target variable via plausible psychological processes."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6487126752710991, 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes Loneliness (UCLS) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination, characterized by repetitive negative thinking, can heighten feelings of loneliness by reinforcing perceptions of social isolation and negative social experiences. Conversely, reducing rumination can decrease loneliness. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where persistent self-focused rumination amplifies social withdrawal and perceived social disconnection.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.012016277939591763, 'perplexity': 1.3975210432720078, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7939271108267694, 'min_prob': 0.07991424879034814, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7116998723584159, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0044702173552555275, 'prob_variance': 0.07561091972227538, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.091063401950702, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 9.009605268679721e-05, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3215324381024009, 'avg_nll': 0.3346999837312507}

