 to inhibit these neural circuits, thereby resulting in greater rumination. This direct neurobiological suppression from social support accounts for the observed inverse relationship.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.003977292495026933, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8046300047513896, 'min_prob': 0.01672530042888471, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6951028160654767, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004312204514546691, 'prob_variance': 0.07849682349753304, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00032217951209028014, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3102701098169492, 'avg_nll': 0.3744701398692413, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3086118623422167, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes rumination (CERQ-R) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces the tendency for rumination by providing emotional resources and alternative coping strategies. Conversely, decreased social support can increase rumination as individuals lack external support to buffer negative thoughts. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the system.', 'prob_std': 0.2801728457533546, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4542206768866288, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0175333892226828}

