   Properties: {'session_id': '97aa030b-2a66-4fd5-a273-ad76ab75dfee', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9432986735362439, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7444250/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpsy/article/PIIS2215-0366(20)30383-7/fulltext'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10451316443330108, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation asserts that depressive symptoms increase loneliness through a direct impairment of social perception, leading individuals to misinterpret their social environment as less supportive, which in turn leads to objectively increased social isolation and thus more loneliness. Step by step:\\n- The link from depressive symptoms to impaired perception of social cues is plausible and logical.\\n- The next step claims that this misperception leads to increased social isolation, which is said to be \\u201cobjective\\u201d and not dependent on actual changes in interaction frequency or behavior.\\n- However, there is a logical inconsistency: if social behavior and interaction frequency do not change, then \\u201cobjective social isolation\\u201d does not actually increase; only the subjective perception of isolation does.\\n- The explanation conflates subjective feelings (loneliness) with objective social isolation, without a clear causal mechanism for how impaired perception alone, absent behavioral change, would increase objective isolation.\\n- It does correctly link depressive symptoms to increased feelings of loneliness via perceptual changes, but errs by asserting this produces objective social isolation \\u201cregardless of\\u201d social behavior."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_pro