116. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'session_id': '97aa030b-2a66-4fd5-a273-ad76ab75dfee', 'judge_perplexity': 799802651677.5496, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10705045732888818, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation posits that sleep disturbance, as measured by PSQI, leads to increased proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha and IL-6).  \\nStep 2: The proposed mechanism is the accumulation of metabolic waste products in the blood due to poor sleep, which supposedly directly stimulates immune cells to secrete proinflammatory cytokines.  \\nStep 3: The explanation explicitly excludes neuroendocrine or autonomic pathways, emphasizing only the metabolic waste mechanism.  \\nStep 4: For the causal logic to be sound, the following links must be valid:  \\n  a) Sleep disturbance leads to impaired clearance of metabolic waste.  \\n  b) Accumulated metabolic waste directly stimulates immune cells to secrete TNF-alpha and IL-6.  \\n  c) This mechanism is the primary driver of the observed positive relationship.  \\nStep 5: Internally, the explanation is logically sound if each step follows from the previous one. The explanation does lay out a sequential process: sleep disturbance \\u2192 metabolic waste buildup \\u2192 immune stimulation \\u2192 increased cytokines.  \\nStep 6: However, the reasoning is partially incomplete because it asserts that the metabolic waste mechanism is primary and dismisses other well-known mechanisms (like neuroendocrine or autonomic changes) without justification. Moreover, it presumes direct immune stimulation by waste products without specifying a plausible intermediary or the type of waste.  \\nStep 7: The causal chain is coherent and plausible in structure, but the argument that this is the main or only pathway is not sufficiently supported by the internal logic (since alternative explanations are dismissed without reasoning).  \\n  \\nTherefore, the explanation is partially correct in its internal logical structure but overstates the exclusivity and primacy of the proposed mechanism.  \\n  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7666531004844418, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6286015764922036, 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes an increase in proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily through the accumulation of metabolic waste products in the bloodstream. According to this mechanism, poor sleep quality impairs the body’s ability to efficiently excrete metabolic byproducts overnight, leading to their buildup. These accumulated waste products act as direct stimulants for immune cells to secrete higher levels of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Thus, the positive relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and proinflammatory processes is explained by the physiological burden of metabolic waste, rather than neuroendocrine or autonomic pathways.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes an increase in proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily through the accumulation of metabolic waste products in the bloodstream. According to this mechanism, poor sleep quality impairs the body’s ability to efficiently excrete metabolic byproducts overnight, leading to their buildup. These accumulated waste products act as direct stimulants for immune cells to secrete higher levels of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Thus, the positive relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and proinflammatory processes is explained by the physiological burden of metabolic waste, rather than neuroendocrine or autonomic pathways.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821237136123625, 'min_prob': 0.02170328799019462, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6971615621127164, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036249097097319784, 'prob_variance': 0.07225041414262147, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0007483091243455594, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3271856618632427, 'avg_nll': 0.3123281134550834, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4520044644459387, 'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct physiological mechanisms. Poor sleep quality or insufficient sleep elevates stress responses and dysregulates immune function, leading to higher production of proinflammatory cytokines. Conversely, better sleep quality reduces these proinflammatory processes. This direct positive causal relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence.', 'prob_std': 0.26879437148612595, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.366603020313073, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0151317710476175}

