   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.388504943945945, 'session_id': '97aa030b-2a66-4fd5-a273-ad76ab75dfee', 'judge_perplexity': 2.0290057574335725, 'prob_std': 0.2870899617709407, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11364912097410909, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109579489362239, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation starts by asserting a direct causal link from prosocial behavior to perceived social support. It provides a plausible mechanism: engaging in prosocial acts can foster reciprocal interactions and strengthen social connections, which could lead to increased perceptions of support. The explanation also addresses the converse relationship, noting that decreased prosocial behavior might reduce perceived support. The logical flow is sound, connecting the act of helping others to the likely outcome of feeling more supported, especially through the mechanisms of reciprocity and connection-building. There are no evident logical or scientific gaps in the reasoning as presented. The explanation stays focused on the internal logic of how prosocial actions could increase perceived support."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5813016432991067, 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in prosocial acts such as helping others or cooperating in group settings can lead individuals to perceive greater social support from their environment. This is because such behaviors often foster reciprocal interactions and strengthen social connections. Conversely, a de