193. Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2964232787501198, 'session_id': '97aa030b-2a66-4fd5-a273-ad76ab75dfee', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8219362014833078, 'prob_std': 0.24542110010760457, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10694668194491844, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/', 'https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8105082013283982, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation asserts a direct causal relationship between proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) and depressive symptoms, stating that elevated cytokines induce neurobiological changes that increase depression likelihood or severity. The explanation also claims that decreasing proinflammatory processes can reduce depressive symptoms, implying a bidirectional effect. The mechanistic link is described in general terms (\\"neurobiological changes\\"), but the explanation does not specify the exact mechanisms or intermediate steps, such as which neurobiological pathways are involved or how these cytokines impact mood-regulating systems. The reasoning is logically coherent: it proposes a cause (elevated cytokines), an effect (depression), and a plausible mediating process (neurobiological changes), and it is clear about the direction of the effect. However, by stating this is a \\"direct causal relationship,\\" the explanation may overstate the directness, as it acknowledges mediation by neurobiological changes. Furthermore, the statement that \\"decreasing proinflammatory processes can correspondingly reduce depressive symptoms\\" assumes reversibility and symmetry of the effect, which may not always hold in causal reasoning without more justification. Overall, the reasoning is sound and coherent, though slightly oversimplified in its language about directness and reversibility."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.602447968630562, 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Elevated levels of these proinflammatory cytokines can induce neurobiological changes that increase the likelihood or severity of depressive symptoms. Decreasing proinflammatory processes can correspondingly reduce depressive symptoms. This relationship is supported by mechanistic evidence linking inflammation to changes in mood and behavior.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.016236168270611355, 'perplexity': 1.294941294910158, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8538027230658207, 'min_prob': 0.03310763331958213, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.680200217500683, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030953807079987664, 'prob_variance': 0.060231516378026866, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8500566023388381, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00021812460482414946, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3270270354954135, 'avg_nll': 0.25846536200826153}

