   Properties: {'session_id': '9329f27d-8aa4-4019-ac78-6201736096e8', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6825899768391173, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09449145475049284, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a specific and internally consistent mechanism (plausibility, coherence) describing how perceived social support might paradoxically heighten loneliness via increased sensitivity to social absence. The direction of causality is mostly clear, with social support posited to precede loneliness (temporality). However, the strength of the relationship is not well established; the explanation frames the effect as possible or paradoxical without conveying that it is a substantial or primary influence. The mechanism, while plausible, is somewhat counterintuitive and may border on speculative, but it avoids circularity and maintains logical consistency."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6270437857417636, 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) influences loneliness (UCLS) primarily through increasing individuals’ awareness of social interactions and relationships. As individuals perceive higher levels of social support, they become more attuned to the presence and expectations of others, which enhances their sensitivity to periods of solitude or social absence. This heightened sensitivity can paradoxically lead to greater feelings of loneliness (UCLS) because individuals with high perceived social support are more likely to notice and be affected by occasional lapses in social contact, thereby increasing their reported loneliness even in generally supportive environments.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) influences loneliness (UCLS) primarily through increasing individuals’ awareness of social interactions and relationships. As individuals perceive higher levels of social support, they become more attuned to the presence and expectations of others, which enhances their sensitivity to periods of solitude or social absence. This heightened sensitivity can paradoxically lead to greater feelings of loneliness (UCLS) because individuals with high perceived social support are more likely to notice and be affected by occasional lapses in social contact, thereby increasing their reported loneliness even in generally supportive environments.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.04148920420197757, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8155938334994569, 'min_prob': 0.01538897804949971, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7052300131418349, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005002949804000342, 'prob_variance': 0.07812071622081258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0023811921750671225, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3073946238152073, 'avg_nll': 0.34288038320667324, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2785669964570157, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence, and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided set.', 'prob_std': 0.279500834025254, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4090002117588092, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0665909514197354}

