   Properties: {'session_id': '9329f27d-8aa4-4019-ac78-6201736096e8', 'judge_perplexity': 1.738286250369971, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3219878/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10068132465859668, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible and specific causal mechanism (neurological suppression via social interactions) and maintains internal coherence and logical consistency. Temporal direction is clearly specified, with MSPSS preceding and affecting rumination. However, the claim overstates the immediacy and independence from all other factors (e.g., personality or cognitive style) without justification, and the neurobiological pathway is asserted rather than convincingly explained, limiting plausibility. The strength of the relationship is implied to be substantial, but the lack of nuance and overgeneralization reduces confidence in the overall account."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7488358030961512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.610462739683341, 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on rumination (CERQ-R) because individuals who perceive higher social support are more likely to engage in frequent social interactions, which directly suppresses the neurological pathways responsible for repetitive negative thinking. As a result, increased MSPSS leads to immediate reductions in CERQ-R, independent of any underlying personality traits or cognitive styles. Lower levels of MSPSS, by contrast, fail to inhibit these neural circuits, thereby resulting in greater rumination. This direct neuro