   Properties: {'session_id': '9329f27d-8aa4-4019-ac78-6201736096e8', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5846557217312967, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09289129816743663, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation fails on PLAUSIBILITY by proposing a mechanism (IL-10 as an energy substrate) that is not scientifically plausible, as anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 are not metabolized for energy. TEMPORALITY is addressed (sleep disturbance precedes IL-10 decrease), and the direction is clear, but the implausible mechanism undermines causal interpretation. STRENGTH is implied but not justified, and COHERENCE is compromised by the internally inconsistent and biologically inaccurate reasoning. Overall, the explanation is undermined by its implausible and incoherent causal mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7587186951846719, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6598387568465358, 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) leads to a decrease in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because fragmented sleep places the body in a state of heightened metabolic activity, which directly consumes circulating IL-10 as an alternative energy substrate. As a result, individuals experiencing increased sleep disturbance utilize more IL-10 for metabolic demands, thereby lowering its concentration in the body. Conversely, when sleep quality improves, metabolic demands normalize and the body conserves IL-10, resulting in higher levels. This mechanism suggests that the relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is mediated by the metabolic consumption of anti-inflammatory cytokines during periods of poor sleep.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) leads to a decrease in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because fragmented sleep places the body in a state of heightened metabolic activity, which directly consumes circulating IL-10 as an alternative energy substrate. As a result, individuals experiencing increased sleep disturbance utilize more IL-10 for metabolic demands, thereby lowering its concentration in the body. Conversely, when sleep quality improves, metabolic demands normalize and the body conserves IL-10, resulting in higher levels. This mechanism suggests that the relationship between sleep disturbance (PSQI) and anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is mediated by the metabolic consumption of anti-inflammatory cytokines during periods of poor sleep.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.02231212158658944, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8041494334056366, 'min_prob': 0.027627787218500427, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.737447102949813, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004363812428879458, 'prob_variance': 0.08373745764831059, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0015658904154749807, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3047807378549974, 'avg_nll': 0.3649332792901754, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3091721046365241, 'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased sleep disturbance is linked with a reduction in IL-10 production, as poor sleep impairs anti-inflammatory immune function. Conversely, improved sleep quality can increase IL-10 levels. This relationship is supported by evidence showing sleep disruption leads to suppressed anti-inflammatory cytokine activity.', 'prob_std': 0.2893742518751635, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.440417899